d is actually running or if there are any deployment issues. By checking the pods, they can see if there are any crashes or restarts, and look at the deployment status to ensure the correct number of replicas are running. If the user-service isn\'t listed, that\'s a clear sign of a problem. If it is listed but in a crash loop, that points to a different issue. This step is crucial to confirm the current state of the service before diving deeper into metrics or traces.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl get pods -n social-network"  \nTo verify if the user-service pod is running, I need to check the current state of pods in the social-network namespace. This will confirm whether the service is deployed and operational, or if there are issues like crashes, restarts, or deployment failures. This is a critical first step to validate the service\'s existence and health before further investigation.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': Usage(completion_tokens=387, prompt_tokens=1661, total_tokens=2048, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc9c-5924-7f60-8dc4-41aa2e0ad78f-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1661, 'output_tokens': 387, 'total_tokens': 2048}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='5146c081-7194-41a2-9a16-88be1d2be8cd')]]
